<!--
    BM-005 — Budget Alert Threshold Evaluation Cron (R-06)
    ======================================================

    Declares the ``ir.cron`` scheduled action that periodically
    evaluates every confirmed budget line's consumption against the
    configured threshold percentages (75 %, 90 %, 100 %, 110 %) and
    creates ``budget.alert`` records for newly-crossed thresholds.

    R-06 Compliance
    ===============
    The scheduled evaluation is declared here as a data-file XML
    ``ir.cron`` record (NOT via a Python-level scheduler such as
    ``threading.Timer`` or ``APScheduler``). This is the canonical
    Odoo pattern and is mandated by AAP Rule R-06. This record is the
    SOLE scheduling mechanism for BM-005 — no Python-level scheduler
    appears anywhere in the ``account_budget_management`` module.

    Contract With ``budget.alert._cron_evaluate_thresholds``
    ========================================================
    The ``code`` field invokes ``model._cron_evaluate_thresholds()``.
    The method name MUST match exactly the ``@api.model`` method
    defined on ``budget.alert`` in
    ``models/budget_alert.py`` (see its schema ``members_exposed``
    list). Renaming one side of this contract without renaming the
    other will cause the cron run to fail with ``AttributeError`` at
    invocation time.

    Schedule
    ========
    Hourly evaluation (``interval_number=1``,
    ``interval_type='hours'``) satisfies BM-005 constraint C-012:
    notification delivery within 1 hour of a threshold breach. A
    daily cadence would violate this SLA.

    Precedent
    =========
    Pattern mirrors ``addons/mail/data/ir_cron_data.xml`` (OCA
    preferred ``<data noupdate="1">`` wrapper so administrator
    customizations of the cron schedule are preserved across module
    upgrades) with the ``state='code'`` + ``model._method()``
    invocation style observed in
    ``addons/account/data/service_cron.xml``.

    Execution Context
    =================
    The ``user_id`` is set to ``base.user_root`` so the cron runs
    with superuser context across every company's budgets. The
    multi-company ``ir.rule`` records in
    ``security/budget_security.xml`` would otherwise scope the search
    to the executing user's company list, preventing the cron from
    evaluating every confirmed budget system-wide. This is the
    standard pattern for system-level crons observed in both
    ``addons/mail/data/ir_cron_data.xml`` and
    ``addons/account/data/service_cron.xml``.
-->
<odoo>
    <data noupdate="1">

        <record id="ir_cron_budget_alert_evaluation" model="ir.cron">
            <field name="name">Budget Alert Threshold Evaluation</field>
            <field name="model_id" ref="model_budget_alert"/>
            <field name="state">code</field>
            <field name="code">model._cron_evaluate_thresholds()</field>
            <field name="user_id" ref="base.user_root"/>
            <field name="interval_number">1</field>
            <field name="interval_type">hours</field>
            <field name="nextcall"
                   eval="(DateTime.now().replace(minute=0, second=0) + timedelta(hours=1)).strftime('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')"/>
            <field name="active" eval="True"/>
            <field name="priority">5</field>
        </record>

    </data>
</odoo>
